// SASLprep (RFC 4013): what a password has to go through before it is
// hashed.
//
// `packages/postgres` speaks SCRAM, and SCRAM says the password is
// SASLprepped first. So does every other SASL mechanism worth using, and so
// does the `AUTHENTICATE` step of modern IMAP and SMTP. The reason is not
// tidiness: two people typing what they believe is the same password on two
// keyboards can produce different bytes — a non-breaking space instead of a
// space, a precomposed `é` instead of an `e` and an accent, a fullwidth
// letter from a Japanese IME. Without preparation one of those logins
// fails and nobody can say why.
//
// The steps, which are nameprep's with two differences:
//
//   1. **Map.** Non-ASCII spaces become an ordinary space; the characters
//      that map to nothing are removed. Note what is *not* here: no case
//      folding. A password is case-sensitive and `idna`'s preparation is
//      not the same function.
//   2. **Normalise, NFKC.**
//   3. **Prohibit.** Control characters, the non-characters, private use,
//      anything that changes text direction, and — unlike nameprep — plain
//      ASCII control characters as well.
//   4. **Check direction.** A string with right-to-left characters may not
//      also contain left-to-right ones, and must begin and end
//      right-to-left.
//
// There is one more rule, and it is the one that catches people: a
// *stored* string may not contain an unassigned code point, while a *query*
// string may. The reason is that a password stored today must still verify
// after a Unicode update assigns that code point some behaviour. `prepare`
// takes which kind it is being given rather than guessing.
//
// The tables are generated from Python's own `stringprep`, and the
// normalisation is `unicodenorm`'s — which is current where RFC 3454 names
// Unicode 3.2. For a password made of letters, digits and punctuation the
// two agree; the gate says how many inputs it had to leave out.

import unicodenorm

fn table_space() -> str {
    return "a0-a0 1680-1680 2000-200b 202f-202f 205f-205f 3000-3000 "
}

fn table_delete() -> str {
    return "ad-ad 34f-34f 1806-1806 180b-180d 200b-200d 2060-2060 " +
    "fe00-fe0f feff-feff "
}

fn table_prohibited() -> str {
    return "0-1f 7f-a0 340-341 6dd-6dd 70f-70f 1680-1680 180e-180e " +
    "2000-200f 2028-202f 205f-2063 206a-206f 2ff0-2ffb 3000-3000 " +
    "d800-f8ff fdd0-fdef feff-feff fff9-ffff 1d173-1d17a " +
    "1fffe-1ffff 2fffe-2ffff 3fffe-3ffff 4fffe-4ffff 5fffe-5ffff " +
    "6fffe-6ffff 7fffe-7ffff 8fffe-8ffff 9fffe-9ffff afffe-affff " +
    "bfffe-bffff cfffe-cffff dfffe-dffff e0001-e0001 e0020-e007f " +
    "efffe-10ffff "
}

